<p>Anthropic is moving Claude toward a single work surface. On September 16, 2026, the company combined Claude chat and Cowork into one interface and introduced Claude Docs and Claude Slides in beta. Claude Design is also being brought into conversations. The practical change is simple: users no longer have to decide first whether a task belongs in a normal chat or a longer-running workspace.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/claude-docs-slides-workflow-2026.svg" width="1200" height="630" alt="Claude workflow from chat and research to documents and presentations"></figure>
<h2>What changed in Claude</h2><p>Reuters reported that Anthropic is combining chat and Cowork and adding document and presentation tools. The rollout starts with Pro and Max subscribers across web, desktop and mobile, with Team and Free plans to follow. Claude Docs and Claude Slides are beta features, so availability can vary by account and rollout stage.</p><p>The important workflow change is automatic routing. A user can start with a question, continue into research, and then ask Claude to turn the result into a document or presentation without moving the work to a separate product.</p>
<h2>Claude Docs for research and writing</h2><p>Claude Docs is useful when the output needs to become a real working document rather than a chat response. A practical workflow is to begin with the goal, collect evidence, create a structured draft, review claims and then ask Claude to improve the document while you keep control over important facts.</p><p>Anthropic says documents can be exported to formats such as Google Docs and Microsoft Word. That makes the feature more useful for teams that already use a normal document review process.</p>
<h2>Claude Slides for turning evidence into a deck</h2><p>Slides are often where good research becomes weak communication. Instead of asking for a generic presentation at the start, use the research as the source material. First decide the audience and decision the deck should support. Then ask Claude to group the evidence into a small number of messages, create the slide structure and flag claims that need citations.</p><p>Claude Slides can be edited and presented inside Claude and exported to PowerPoint or PDF, according to the launch reporting. Treat the export as a draft, not as the final quality gate. Check numbers, chart labels, source links, speaker notes and brand requirements before sending it.</p>
<h2>A better end-to-end workflow</h2><ol><li><strong>Start with the outcome.</strong> Write what the document or deck needs to help someone decide or do.</li><li><strong>Build an evidence list.</strong> Separate confirmed facts, estimates, opinions and open questions.</li><li><strong>Research in the same conversation.</strong> Keep important source links near the claims they support.</li><li><strong>Create the document.</strong> Ask Claude to turn the evidence into a structured working draft.</li><li><strong>Review before formatting.</strong> Fix factual gaps and unsupported statements before polishing the output.</li><li><strong>Create slides from the approved document.</strong> Do not let visual polish hide weak evidence.</li><li><strong>Export and inspect.</strong> Check the final file outside Claude before delivery.</li></ol>
<h2>Where human review still matters</h2><p>The unified interface makes agentic work easier to reach, but it does not remove the need for review. A longer task can involve files, tools, connectors or external actions. For important work, review the instructions Claude is using, the sources it relied on and any action that changes external systems.</p><p>Enterprise teams should also check administrator controls before enabling beta features. The rollout is staged, and Anthropic says Enterprise administrators can control when these capabilities are enabled.</p>
<h2>What changes for SEO and marketing teams</h2><p>For marketers, the biggest opportunity is reducing the handoff between research, content and presentation work. A single conversation can move from search-intent research to a content brief, then to a client document and finally to a presentation. The risk is that one incorrect research assumption can travel through every output.</p><p>A good workflow therefore keeps a fact ledger. Mark each key claim as sourced, inferred or still unknown. Then use the same approved evidence across the document and slides. This is safer than asking the model to independently rewrite the same topic multiple times.</p>
<h2>How this compares with the older Cowork model</h2><p>Cowork previously gave users a separate place for longer-running work. The September change removes that upfront choice. That can reduce friction, but it also means users need to understand when a normal question is turning into a task that may use more tools or run for longer.</p><p>For teams, the right policy is not “never use autonomous work.” It is “match autonomy to impact.” Research, drafting and low-risk formatting can use more freedom. Publishing, financial changes, customer communication and production changes should keep a human approval step.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Are Claude Docs and Slides available to everyone?</h3><p>No. They are beta features rolling out first to Pro and Max users, with other plans following. Enterprise availability is controlled by administrators.</p><h3>Can Claude Slides export to PowerPoint?</h3><p>Yes. Anthropic’s launch coverage says presentations can be exported to PowerPoint or PDF.</p><h3>Does the unified interface mean Cowork is gone?</h3><p>The separate Chat-versus-Cowork choice is being removed as Anthropic combines the experiences into one Claude interface.</p>
